Currently the loop nest is `for ci: for kd: for kh: for kw:`, which means the validity masks (`d_valid`, `h_valid`, `w_valid`), the combined `mask`, and large parts of the address arithmetic (which don't depend on `ci`) are redundantly recomputed `C_IN_PER_GROUP` times per spatial kernel position. By reordering the loop nest to `for kd: for kh: for kw: for ci:`, these invariant quantities are computed once per `(kd,kh,kw)` instead of once per `(ci,kd,kh,kw)`, reducing redundant integer/boolean arithmetic and register pressure in the innermost loop while preserving identical semantics and memory access patterns.

