node 9_20260823-213452 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this kernel, it already implements output/spatial tiling with staged input reuse for the `stride==1` case. However, that staging path uses `tl.dot` with a one-hot "shift matrix" purely to perform a compile-time row selection (since Triton doesn't support arbitrary tensor slicing). This forces a full `(BLOCK_OH x STAGE_H) @ (STAGE_H x BLOCK_OW)` fp32 matmul (`allow_tf32=False`, so no tensor-core benefit) per tap just to shift rows — spending far more FLOPs than the small amount of memory bandwidth it saves for `KH=3`. This is almost certainly why the kernel (8.94ms) is much slower than cuDNN (1.72ms).

The fix: replace the wasteful matmul-based row-shift with direct masked per-tap loads (identical to the already-correct general-stride fallback path), removing the tensor-core detour that provides no benefit here while keeping coalesced, masked global loads.

